# DB state after the fleet's hour: table devices, 5 rows.
#
# Written by: Manager.pollDevice (liveness) → queries.MarkDeviceOnline: status, last_seen, last_error
#             Manager.refreshDeviceInfo (Tier 6) → queries.UpdateDeviceHealth: cpu_load, memory, uptime, last_seen; queries.UpdateDeviceInfo: platform, board, ros_version, firmware_version, architecture
#             Manager.probeClass → queries.SetDeviceClass: device_class, class_probed_at, cpu_count, cpu_mhz_max
#             CPUSampler.sample (Tier 1) → queries.UpdateDeviceLoad: cpu_load, memory_used, uptime
# The hour:   the call-plan fleet of 5 fake devices (callplan_harness_test.go), every scheduled loop,
#             one simulated hour and a minute from 2026-01-05 12:00 UTC; the devices' counters, CPU,
#             memory and uptime move with the clock (callplan_motion_test.go)
#
# A change in this file is a change in what the pollers store.
# Rewrite it with: go test ./internal/poller -run TestDBState -update
#
# Normalised (dbstate_test.go):
#   last_seen: SQLite's CURRENT_TIMESTAMP (wall clock) → <sqlite-now>; stored as TEXT "2006-01-02 15:04:05" (as CURRENT_TIMESTAMP writes it)
#   updated_at: SQLite's CURRENT_TIMESTAMP (wall clock) → <sqlite-now>; stored as TEXT "2006-01-02 15:04:05" (as CURRENT_TIMESTAMP writes it)
#   class_probed_at: the simulated clock, as the offset from the start of the hour; stored as TEXT time.Time.String() ("2006-01-02 15:04:05 +0000 UTC", as the driver binds a time.Time)
#   REAL values with three decimals, strings quoted, NULL as NULL
#   rows ordered by the primary key (id)
#   left out, as only the harness's seed writes them (checked unchanged): address, identity, username, password_enc, use_tls, api_port, tags, notes, created_at, class_override, poll_profile, polling_paused_until, pin_cert_sha256

